<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Fuzz drivers are important components of greybox fuzzing; they encapsulate the target interface of the tested library, define the test space, and largely determine the outcomes of fuzzing.
Fuzz drivers rely on (fuzz) oracles to detect potential bugs.
As a common practice, existing fuzz drivers typically focus on security testing and adopt program crashes as the oracle.
Although general and practical, this practice overlooks the functionality of the library under test, thereby restricting the bug-mining capability of greybox fuzzing.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we present the first study on metamorphic-based fuzz oracle enhancement (MFOE), which aims to improve existing fuzz drivers by incorporating metamorphic-based oracles.
The metamorphic-based oracles are grounded on metamorphic relations (MRs),
which reflect the expected relationships between multiple inputs and their corresponding outputs.
However, both the construction and integration of metamorphic-based oracles require a deep understanding of the fuzz target, making automatic MFOE particularly challenging.
Modern large language models (LLMs), with their advanced capabilities in code understanding and generation, provide a promising opportunity to overcome this obstacle, inspiring us to develop an LLM-based framework.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p">We name the LLM-based MFOE framework as MetaFOE and extensively evaluate it on fuzz drivers selected from OSS-Fuzz.
To conduct a comprehensive investigation, we adopt <span id="abstract1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">three</span> modern LLMs and <span id="abstract1.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">five</span> prompt strategies to configure MetaFOE.
In total, we generate <span id="abstract1.3.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3,475</span> MRs, <span id="abstract1.3.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">77.3%</span> of which are applicable.
Building on these MRs, we implement <span id="abstract1.3.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">12,351</span> meta drivers (i.e., drivers incorporating metamorphic-based oracles), of which <span id="abstract1.3.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">6,228</span> are valid.
After <span id="abstract1.3.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">three</span> hours of fuzzing, these valid meta drivers achieve an average <span id="abstract1.3.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">18.7%</span> improvement in edge coverage and triggered <span id="abstract1.3.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,528</span> unique crashes.
Our study highlights the necessity of incorporating fuzz drivers with metamorphic-based oracles and demonstrates the feasibility of an LLM-based automatic MFOE, offering valuable insight to the fuzzing community.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Fuzzing, Fuzz Driver, Large Language Model, Metamorphic-based Oracle
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Greybox fuzzing is a simple yet super effective automatic testing technique <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Böhme et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
With a favorable trade-off between effectiveness and efficiency, greybox fuzzing has become an important testing capability, empowering notable bug-hunting services <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Serebryany, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Syzbot, 2026</a>)</cite> and leading to the discovery of tens of thousands of software vulnerabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zalewski, 2026</a>)</cite>.
The fuzz driver (or fuzz harness<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          The terms “fuzz driver” and “fuzz harness” are both widely adopted in the fuzzing community and are interchangeable with each other. In this paper, we primarily use the term fuzz driver.</span></span></span>) is a vital component of greybox fuzzing, especially for domains such as library testing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">LLVM-LibFuzzer, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>.
Typically, a fuzz driver functions as the entry point for test executions; it not only encapsulates the units to be tested (e.g., library APIs) but also delimits the reachable code regions, which largely determine the results of fuzzing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lyu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Galland and Böhme, 2025</a>)</cite>.
Substantial manual efforts have been invested in developing and maintaining fuzz drivers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">OSS-Fuzz, 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Görz et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and various automated techniques have been proposed along this line of research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Babić et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Toffalini et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
All of these advances underscore the importance of crafting high-quality fuzz drivers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although numerous works have been devoted to driver generation, they mainly concentrate on the synthesis of test sequences (i.e., ordered and structured API calls <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sherman and Nagy, 2025</a>)</cite>) while largely overlooking the construction of <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fuzz oracles</span>.
In software testing, an <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">oracle</span> is the mechanism used to determine whether an observed program behavior is a bug <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barr et al., 2014</a>)</cite>; and we refer to those employed in fuzzing as fuzz oracles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manes et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.
To understand the current status of the fuzz oracles used in existing drivers, we conduct a systematic inspection (detailed results are in <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1. RQ1: Existing Fuzz Oracles ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§5.1</span></a>) of the source code of <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,068</span> drivers from OSS-Fuzz <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">OSS-Fuzz, 2026a</a>)</cite>.
We find that 63.9% of the drivers <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">do not</span> implement any dedicated bug-detection mechanisms and rely solely on program crashes to indicate bugs.
Among the remaining drivers, 50.1% only implement “pseudo oracles” to check the validity of test inputs and the drivers themselves.
Only 18.9% of the inspected drivers implement functional oracles that target the libraries under test, highlighting the critical need to improve existing fuzz oracles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we conduct the first study investigating the enhancement of fuzz oracles, in particular those embedded in existing drivers.
In the prior art, researchers have recognized the shortcomings of fuzz oracles and have proposed novel techniques to tackle this problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Serebryany et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han and Zhou, 2020</a>)</cite>. As a powerful technique for alleviating the oracle problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Segura et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2013</a>)</cite>, metamorphic testing has naturally attracted the attention of the fuzzing community and has been adopted to enhance fuzz oracles.
For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al. (2024)</a></cite> summarize the characteristics of excessive data exposures (EDEs) and design novel oracles to fuzz and expose EDEs in real-world web applications.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kokkonis et al. (2025)</a></cite> identify the symptoms of code-level backdoors and construct a metamorphic (test) oracle to assist AFL++ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">AFL++Team, 2026</a>)</cite> in detecting run-time backdoor triggers.
These works (which can be generally termed <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">metamorphic fuzzing</span>) have proposed insightful solutions to the fuzz oracle problem, yet only concentrate on constructing oracles to expose specific types of bugs (i.e., EDEs and code-level backdoors) and do not address the lack of well-crafted oracles in existing drivers, which motivates us to explore this issue.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by the success of metamorphic fuzzing, we propose improving existing fuzz drivers with metamorphic oracles.
We refer to this pipeline as metamorphic-based fuzz oracle enhancement (MFOE) and identify two essential tasks of it:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metamorphic Relation Generation (MRG)</span>.
Analyzing fuzzed libraries to extract metamorphic relations (MRs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, which are the core of metamorphic testing and the foundation for constructing metamorphic oracles.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fuzz Oracle Implementation (FOI)</span>.
Analyzing given fuzz drivers, matching them with the generated MRs, and implementing appropriate oracles to construct enhanced drivers.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Both of MRG and FOI require a deep understanding of the input software assets (i.e., fuzzed libraries and fuzz drivers), which poses a tricky obstacle to MFOE.
In recent years, modern large language models (LLMs) have advanced substantially and shown strong capabilities in code generation and understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Valentin et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, which opens opportunities to mitigate the obstacle to MFOE.
Moreover, pioneer researchers have demonstrated that LLMs can be applied to the generation of fuzz drivers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lyu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and MRs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, which inspires us to develop an LLM-based workflow for MFOE.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We name the LLM-based MFOE pipeline as MetaFOE and conduct extensive experiments to gain an empirical understanding of it.
Specifically, we systematically evaluate the capabilities of LLMs in (1) generating valid MRs for given fuzz drivers and target libraries, and (2) constructing metamorphic oracles based on valid MRs and improving given fuzz drivers;
in this evaluation, <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">three</span> representative LLMs are adopted, and <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">five</span> mainstream prompting strategies are investigated.
For each generated MR, we manually validate it following pioneering work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.
For each enhanced driver candidate produced by an LLM, we verify its validity through a three-phase process with checks at both the compilation and runtime levels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">With 10 original fuzz drivers and the corresponding project documents as raw input, MetaFOE generates <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3,475</span> MRs and <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">12,351</span> meta drivers in total, of which <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">77.3%</span> and <span id="S1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">50.4%</span> are applicable and valid.
For the <span id="S1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">6,228</span> valid meta drivers, we further evaluate their fuzzing capabilities (i.e., code coverage and crash detection) through <span id="S1.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">three</span>-hour campaigns.
The results show that the meta drivers achieve <span id="S1.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">18.7%</span> more edge coverage and trigger <span id="S1.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,528</span> unique crashes.
These results reveal that metamorphic oracles can not only improve code coverage but also crash detection.
We also experiment MetaFOE using <span id="S1.p6.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">three</span> LLMs and <span id="S1.p6.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">five</span> prompt strategies to evaluate its sensitivity to different configurations.
The main contributions of this paper are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Novel Pipeline</span>.
We propose a novel task named metamorphic-based fuzz oracle enhancement, and develop an LLM-based pipeline MetaFOE to realize it.
To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to improve fuzz oracles encoded in existing drivers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Systematic Study</span>.
We conduct a systematic study of existing fuzz oracles by inspecting <span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,068</span> drivers from OSS-Fuzz.
We also perform extensive experiments to evaluate (1) MetaFOE’s performance in MRG and FOI, which consume <span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">213 M</span> tokens; and (2) the fuzzing capability of valid meta drivers, which spends more than <span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">two</span> CPU years.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Enhanced Drivers</span>.
Through extensive experiments, MetaFOE generates <span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">6,228</span> valid meta drivers, which cover <span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">18.7%</span> more edges than original drivers on average, and trigger <span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,528</span> crashes.
We release these generated meta drivers in our artifact <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">MetaFOE-Team, 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Background</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides background knowledge on metamorphic-based fuzz oracle enhancement, including greybox fuzzing, metamorphic testing, and the large language model.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Greybox Fuzzing</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Greybox fuzzing is a representative modern software security technique responsible for numerous bugs.
Building on the basic fuzzing workflow, greybox fuzzing additionally leverages code coverage as the primary feedback.
It continuously generates a large number of test inputs to repeatedly exercise fuzz targets (invoked through fuzz drivers), aiming to explore previously unseen code regions or trigger latent bugs via massive executions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qian et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kokkonis et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Fuzz Driver</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The fuzz driver is a critical component of greybox fuzzing that implements the execution entry point of the fuzz target (e.g., the library under test).
In addition to the <span id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">main</span> step that invokes the Abstract Programming Interfaces (APIs) to be fuzzed, a qualified fuzz driver usually includes two other steps:
(1) a <span id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">preprocessing</span> step to prepare test inputs for the tested interfaces, and (2) a <span id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> postprocessing</span> step to release the resources <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">LLVM-LibFuzzer, 2026</a>)</cite>.
<a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Fuzz Driver ‣ 2.1. Greybox Fuzzing ‣ 2. Background ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 1</span></a> illustrates two LibFuzzer-style drivers from the C-Blosc2 project; the code blocks that implement the preprocessing, main, and postprocessing steps are colored orange, green, and blue.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our study, we focus on fuzz oracles and dissect a driver into two parts: the <span id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">test sequence</span> and the <span id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">test oracle</span>.
Specifically, the test sequence part defines the test logic of the tested APIs (i.e., the invocation order and parameter dependencies), and the test oracle part validates the execution results of the sequence to determine whether a bug is observed.
The driver shown in <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Fuzz Driver ‣ 2.1. Greybox Fuzzing ‣ 2. Background ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 1</span></a> encompasses both the test sequence and the test oracle parts, where the explicit oracles<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
              Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barr et al., 2014</a>)</cite>, we distinguish between <span id="footnote2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">explicit oracles</span>, which explicitly check program properties (e.g., assertions), and <span id="footnote2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">implicit oracles</span>, which capture properties that should <span id="footnote2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> be violated, such as program crashes.</span></span></span> implemented with <span id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert</span> statements are highlighted in red.
C-Blosc2 is a C library for data compression, and the <span id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert</span> oracles embedded in the driver <span id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">checksum_fuzzer</span> are designed to verify the equivalence and consistency of CRC32, which are fundamental building blocks in data compression.
With such oracles, a driver can help fuzzing to detect not only plain bugs that lead to program crashes, but also functional bugs that violate the semantic correctness of critical APIs, such as CRC32 implemented in the C-Blosc2 library.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, unlike the <span id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert</span> oracles illustrated in <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Fuzz Driver ‣ 2.1. Greybox Fuzzing ‣ 2. Background ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 1</span></a>, the common practice in fuzzing still largely relies on program crashes (i.e., implicit oracles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barr et al., 2014</a>)</cite>) as the sole oracle.
As shown in <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Fuzz Driver ‣ 2.1. Greybox Fuzzing ‣ 2. Background ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 1</span></a>, the driver <span id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fuzz_compress_chunk</span> does not perform any explicit checks on the execution results; it just constructs inputs, invokes the target APIs (e.g., <span id="S2.SS1.SSSx1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">blosc1_set_compressor</span>), and frees the allocated memory, relying on repeated executions of this workflow during fuzzing to expose bugs.
Such implicit oracles are overly simplistic and may miss important bugs, revealing limitations in existing fuzz oracles.
This observation motivates us to carry out this study.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure" aria-describedby="acmlabel1">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S2.F1.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:169.1pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14164v1/driver-example-withoracle.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:234/268;" width="234" height="268"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.fig1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S2.F1.fig1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Driver <span id="S2.F1.fig1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">checksum_fuzzer</span> implementing oracles with <span id="S2.F1.fig1.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert</span> statements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">C-Blosc2, 2026a</a>)</cite>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S2.F1.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:169.1pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14164v1/driver-example-withoutoracle.svg" id="S2.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:234/268;" width="234" height="268"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S2.F1.fig2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Driver <span id="S2.F1.fig2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fuzz_compress_chunk</span> implementing no explicit oracles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">C-Blosc2, 2026b</a>)</cite>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Two LibFuzzer-style fuzz drivers from C-Blosc2, a project integrated in OSS-Fuzz.</span><span id="acmlabel1" class="ltx_nodisplay ltx_acm_description">Two LibFuzzer-style fuzz drivers from C-Blosc2, a project integrated in \ossfuzz{}</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">OSS-Fuzz</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">OSS-Fuzz is a continuous fuzzing service provided by Google to support open-source projects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">OSS-Fuzz, 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google, 2026</a>)</cite>.
With incentives in place, an open-source library can choose to participate in OSS-Fuzz for continuous fuzzing.
To enable integration into the OSS-Fuzz infrastructure, library vendors should prepare standard artifacts mandated by OSS-Fuzz (e.g., integration configurations and fuzz drivers) before officially requesting inclusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">OSS-Fuzz, 2026b</a>)</cite>.
To date, OSS-Fuzz has successfully integrated more than 1,000 projects, supporting the fuzzing of libraries written in mainstream languages such as C/C++, Java, and Python <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">OSS-Fuzz, 2026c</a>)</cite>.
By aggregating abundant fuzzing assets (e.g., initial seed corpora, fuzz-exposed bugs, and fuzz drivers) from actively maintained open-source projects, OSS-Fuzz has become a valuable research testbed, facilitating a number of studies in areas such as regression fuzzing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu and Böhme, 2021</a>)</cite>, CI/CD fuzzing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang and Lemieux, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and empirical fuzzing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding and Le Goues, 2021</a>)</cite>.
Following this line of research, we adopt OSS-Fuzz as our primary data source and conduct this empirical study with the fuzz oracles integrated in OSS-Fuzz.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Metamorphic Testing</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Metamorphic testing was first proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (1998)</a></cite> to address the test generation problem.
During the past few decades, it has evolved into a prominent remediation of the test oracle problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.
Given an algorithm <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the design specification) and a program <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> that implements this algorithm, metamorphic testing aims to extract a set of metamorphic relations (MRs) <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\{\mathcal{R}_{1},\mathcal{R}_{2},...,\mathcal{R}_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\{\mathcal{R}_{1},\mathcal{R}_{2},...,\mathcal{R}_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and leverage them to validate whether <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is implemented correctly.
Essentially, an MR <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a necessary property that the algorithm <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> (and thus its implementation <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>) should satisfy.
If an input–output pair violates <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, then the output is deemed erroneous, indicating the presence of bugs in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> denote a concrete input and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the corresponding output produced by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.
Once an MR <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}=\langle x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n},f(x_{1}),f(x_{2}),\ldots,f(x_{n})\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}=\langle x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n},f(x_{1}),f(x_{2}),\ldots,f(x_{n})\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is identified and established, metamorphic testing typically proceeds in four steps to generate test cases and validate the correctness of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, as follows:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Construct <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the MR used to validate <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a sequence of source test cases <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{k}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{k}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, execute the program <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> on each test case to obtain the corresponding outputs <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle p(x_{1}),p(x_{2}),\ldots,p(x_{k})\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle p(x_{1}),p(x_{2}),\ldots,p(x_{k})\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Construct the follow-up test cases <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle x_{k+1},x_{k+2},\ldots,x_{n}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle x_{k+1},x_{k+2},\ldots,x_{n}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> according to <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, execute them to obtain the corresponding outputs <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle p(x_{k+1}),p(x_{k+2}),\ldots,p(x_{n})\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle p(x_{k+1}),p(x_{k+2}),\ldots,p(x_{n})\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Validate <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> by checking whether the execution results, i.e., <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle p(x_{k+1}),p(x_{k+2}),\ldots,p(x_{n})\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle p(x_{k+1}),p(x_{k+2}),\ldots,p(x_{n})\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfy <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; if <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is violated, then a bug in <math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is revealed.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we propose enhancing existing fuzz drivers by integrating metamorphic oracles, which are derived and implemented based on MRs.
The programs under test are the libraries invoked by the fuzz drivers, with test generation handled by the fuzzer.
The MRs should therefore be extracted from both the libraries under test and the associated drivers, and we address this task using large language models (LLMs).
Next, we further dissect the tasks involved in fuzz oracle enhancement in <a href="#S3" title="3. Metamorphic-based Fuzz Oracle Enhancement ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§3</span></a>, and elaborate on the proposed LLM-based workflow in <a href="#S4" title="4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Metamorphic-based Fuzz Oracle Enhancement</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modern greybox fuzzing typically relies on the program crash—a type of implicit oracle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barr et al., 2014</a>)</cite>—to identify bugs.
This practice restricts the range of bugs that can potentially be detected, as crash-only oracles inherently overlook the functional semantics of the fuzz targets, i.e., the libraries bridged by the drivers.
To address this limitation, we propose <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">metamorphic-based fuzz oracle enhancement</span> (MFOE), a novel pipeline that augments existing fuzz drivers with metamorphic oracles extracted from the libraries under test.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\langle\mathcal{T},\mathcal{O}_{i}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\langle\mathcal{T},\mathcal{O}_{i}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> be a fuzz driver constructed according to typical fuzzing practice, where <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents an implicit oracle that monitors unintended program crashes.
The goal of MFOE is to modify <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\prime}=\langle\mathcal{T},\mathcal{O}_{e}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\prime}=\langle\mathcal{T},\mathcal{O}_{e}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing the original <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with an explicit oracle <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is constructed based on a metamorphic relation (MR).
Drawing on the metamorphic testing workflow described in <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2. Metamorphic Testing ‣ 2. Background ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§2.2</span></a>, we identify three essential tasks in MFOE, as follows:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MR Extraction</span>.
Let <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be the library behind the fuzzer driver <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The MR extraction task is defined as a mapping <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\mathcal{L})=R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\mathcal{L})=R</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\{\mathcal{R}_{1},\mathcal{R}_{2},...,\mathcal{R}_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\{\mathcal{R}_{1},\mathcal{R}_{2},...,\mathcal{R}_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of extracted MRs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Oracle Instantiation</span>.
To function as a proper oracle (e.g., an assertion), an MR needs to be instantiated in the context of the fuzzer driver.
Let <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be a driver and <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be an MR extracted from the library exercised by <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The oracle instantiation task is defined as a mapping <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\mathcal{D},\mathcal{R})=\mathcal{O}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\mathcal{D},\mathcal{R})=\mathcal{O}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> that crafts and outputs a metamorphic-based explicit oracle <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Oracle Integration</span>.
The last step of MFOE is to integrate the constructed oracle <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> into the original driver <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As such, the oracle integration task is defined as a mapping <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(\mathcal{D},\mathcal{O}_{e})=\mathcal{D}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(\mathcal{D},\mathcal{O}_{e})=\mathcal{D}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\prime}=\langle\mathcal{T},\mathcal{O}_{e}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\prime}=\langle\mathcal{T},\mathcal{O}_{e}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is the modified driver that incorporates a metamorphic-based oracle <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the rest of the paper, we refer to <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">meta driver</span> since it is modified based on MRs.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">An LLM-based Implementation of MFOE</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All three tasks outlined above require a deep understanding of the fuzzed libraries and the original drivers, posing significant challenges to implementing MFOE.
As LLMs have demonstrated extraordinary capabilities in code-relevant tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ni, 2025</a>)</cite>, we are motivated to leverage LLMs to address challenges in MFOE.
Specifically, we develop MetaFOE, an LLM-based framework that implements FOE in two phases.
The first phase is <span id="S3.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">metamorphic relation generation</span> (MRG), which implements the mapping <math id="S3.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> defined in the MR extraction task.
The second phase is <span id="S3.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fuzz oracle implementation</span> (FOI), which implements both the <math id="S3.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> mappings defined in the oracle instantiation and integration tasks.
The reason for handling <math id="S3.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> together is that they share <math id="S3.SS0.SSSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> as input and rely on similar contextual information, such as invoked APIs, relevant variables, and verifiable results.
We further elaborate on MetaFOE in <a href="#S4" title="4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.14164v1/study-framework.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/204;" width="476" height="204" alt="Overview of the study.">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of the study.</span><span id="acmlabel2" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">Overview of the study.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Study Design</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ An LLM-based Implementation of MFOE ‣ 3. Metamorphic-based Fuzz Oracle Enhancement ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 2</span></a> provides an overview of the research questions (RQs), workflows, and subjects (i.e., fuzz drivers, LLMs, and prompt strategies) involved in our study, along with their interrelationships.
Specifically, the upper half of <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ An LLM-based Implementation of MFOE ‣ 3. Metamorphic-based Fuzz Oracle Enhancement ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 2</span></a> illustrates the workflow of MetaFOE, while the lower half exhibits the LLMs and the prompt strategies included in our study.
Each rectangular frame indicates a working stage (in blue) or a group of study subjects (in gray), where the investigated RQs are presented in the heads of the frames.
Next, we delve into the details of each component.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>MetaFOE</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Taking fuzz assets (e.g., fuzz drivers, seeds, and the source code and documentation of the libraries under test) as input, MetaFOE performs two working phases, namely MRG and FOI (described in <a href="#S3" title="3. Metamorphic-based Fuzz Oracle Enhancement ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§3</span></a>), in sequence to modify given fuzz drivers.
During this procedure, a semi-automatic evaluation is conducted to assess the generated MRs in terms of (1) <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uniqueness</span> and (2) <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">applicability</span>, and an in-process evaluation is conducted to assess the meta drivers along three dimensions: (1) <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">compilability</span>, (2) <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">validity</span>, and (3) <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fuzzing capabilities</span> (i.e., code coverage and bug discovery).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1. </span>Metamorphic Relation Generation (MRG)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the MRG phase, we first prompt an LLM to synthesize MRs, typically producing multiple candidates.
The detailed prompting process for MRG is described in <a href="#S4.SS5.SSS2" title="4.5.2. Prompting for MRG ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§4.5.2</span></a>.
Since some of the generated MRs are redundant or not suitable for oracle construction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, we take two steps to evaluate them and discard those that are inapplicable.
To this end, we first deduplicate the MRs using a similarity-based method powered by a small language model<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
              Similarities are computed using embeddings generated by: <a href="https://huggingface.co/sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2</a></span></span></span>.
After that, we manually validate the applicability of the MRs following the methodology of pioneering work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.
To ensure reliability, the manual validation is carried out by two authors who are experienced in metamorphic testing.
We carefully analyze the features of the libraries under test and validate the corresponding MRs accordingly.
An MR is kept only if both authors agree on its applicability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2. </span>Fuzz Oracle Implementation (FOI)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the MRs retained from the MRG phase, the FOI phase first modifies the original drivers by implementing and injecting new oracles, and then checks the validity of the modified drivers.
<a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1.2. Fuzz Oracle Implementation (FOI) ‣ 4.1. MetaFOE ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 3</span></a> provides a closer view of the workflow in FOI phase, which comprises four stages:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt-driven Oracle Constrution</span>.
Given a prompt template, a fuzz driver, and an MR as input, we first craft concrete prompting instructions to elicit the LLM to generate candidate meta drivers.
In addition to the input described above, the prompting can also take compilation error messages into account if any candidate meta driver has failed in a previous compilation.
We describe the prompting process of FOI in more detail in <a href="#S4.SS5.SSS3" title="4.5.3. Prompting for FOI ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§4.5.3</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compilation-level Driver Validation</span>.
This stage aims to filter out meta drivers that cannot be compiled.
As shown in previous studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schäfer et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, LLMs often struggle to generate compilable code.
To alleviate this limitation, a widely adopted approach is to iteratively reprompt LLMs to refine the outputs, where compilation error messages are typically used to guide the reprompting.
Following this approach, we develop a “reprompt-and-recompile” iteration to produce as many compilable drivers as possible.
We set the number of compilations to at most <span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">five</span> to balance effectiveness and cost.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Runtime-level Driver Validation</span>.
This stage aims to further filter out meta drivers that are compilable yet invalid at runtime, since a valid meta driver is expected to be not only runnable but also stable.
After compiling a meta driver, we first run it against seed inputs to ensure that it is runnable.
A short-term fuzzing (set at <span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">five</span> minutes) is then conducted to eliminate candidate drivers that (1) achieve too little coverage, as it often implies that a meta driver implements erroneous behaviors; or (2) trigger excessive crashes, as it often indicates false positives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>.
Specifically, we discard the drivers that (1) crash in more than 0.01% of the executions or (2) achieve less than 10% of the coverage of the original driver during short-term fuzzing.
The coverage of the original driver is the average of five fuzz campaigns.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fuzzing Capability Evaluation</span>:
We finally conduct a long-term fuzzing to evaluate how the meta drivers are capable in code coverage and bug detection.
For every retained meta driver, we first perform a <span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">three</span>-hour campaign to obtain raw fuzzing results.
Based on the results, we then count the coverage achieved by the meta drivers and analyze the triggered crashes.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.14164v1/workflow-phaseB-oopsla.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/212;" aria-describedby="acmlabel3" width="476" height="212" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Workflow of the LLM-based FOI.</span><span id="acmlabel3" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">Workflow of the LLM-based \techphaseB{}.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Research Questions</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Guided by the roadmap shown in <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ An LLM-based Implementation of MFOE ‣ 3. Metamorphic-based Fuzz Oracle Enhancement ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 2</span></a> and centered on the MetaFOE framework, we investigate five research questions (RQs), as detailed below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="63.43" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 63.43" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,63.43) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.12 L 0 58.32 C 0 61.14 2.29 63.43 5.12 63.43 L 472.26 63.43 C 475.08 63.43 477.38 61.14 477.38 58.32 L 477.38 5.12 C 477.38 2.29 475.08 0 472.26 0 L 5.12 0 C 2.29 0 0 2.29 0 5.12 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.66 5.12 L 1.66 58.32 C 1.66 60.23 3.21 61.77 5.12 61.77 L 472.26 61.77 C 474.17 61.77 475.72 60.23 475.72 58.32 L 475.72 5.12 C 475.72 3.21 474.17 1.66 472.26 1.66 L 5.12 1.66 C 3.21 1.66 1.66 3.21 1.66 5.12 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 7.2 11.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.46em;--ltx-fo-height:3.15em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="46.28" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 43.59)" width="462.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.46em;">
<span id="S4.SS2.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RQ1 (Existing Fuzz Oracles <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1. RQ1: Existing Fuzz Oracles ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§5.1</span></a>):<span id="S4.SS2.p2.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Do existing fuzz drivers contain oracles? What types of oracles are typically implemented? What are the purposes of the existing oracles?</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Fuzz oracles are critical to the effectiveness of fuzzing; nevertheless, their significance is often overlooked, and their role has not been systematically studied.
RQ1 aims to understand the current landscape of fuzzing oracles.
To this end, we identify the types of existing fuzz oracles and examine them to understand the intended purposes behind their implementations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="125.01" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 125.01" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,125.01) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.12 L 0 119.89 C 0 122.72 2.29 125.01 5.12 125.01 L 472.26 125.01 C 475.08 125.01 477.38 122.72 477.38 119.89 L 477.38 5.12 C 477.38 2.29 475.08 0 472.26 0 L 5.12 0 C 2.29 0 0 2.29 0 5.12 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.66 5.12 L 1.66 119.89 C 1.66 121.8 3.21 123.35 5.12 123.35 L 472.26 123.35 C 474.17 123.35 475.72 121.8 475.72 119.89 L 475.72 5.12 C 475.72 3.21 474.17 1.66 472.26 1.66 L 5.12 1.66 C 3.21 1.66 1.66 3.21 1.66 5.12 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 7.2 8.58)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.46em;--ltx-fo-height:7.79em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="107.85" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 107.85)" width="462.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.46em;">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RQ2 (Evaluation of LLM-based MRG <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. RQ2: Evaluation of LLM-based MRG ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§5.2</span></a>):<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Is LLM-based MRG feasible? How well can LLMs construct applicable MRs? To what extent can these MRs be used for oracle construction?</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">MRs are the fundamental elements of conducting metamorphic-based fuzz oracle enhancement (MFOE); their quality is crucial to the performance of the produced meta drivers.
Previous work has explored applying LLMs to synthesize MRs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, but their use in MFOE remains unexplored.
To bridge this gap, we design RQ2 to evaluate how well LLMs can synthesize applicable MRs, with a particular focus on their suitability for oracle construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS2.p6.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="127.7" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 127.7" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,127.7) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.12 L 0 122.58 C 0 125.41 2.29 127.7 5.12 127.7 L 472.26 127.7 C 475.08 127.7 477.38 125.41 477.38 122.58 L 477.38 5.12 C 477.38 2.29 475.08 0 472.26 0 L 5.12 0 C 2.29 0 0 2.29 0 5.12 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.66 5.12 L 1.66 122.58 C 1.66 124.49 3.21 126.04 5.12 126.04 L 472.26 126.04 C 474.17 126.04 475.72 124.49 475.72 122.58 L 475.72 5.12 C 475.72 3.21 474.17 1.66 472.26 1.66 L 5.12 1.66 C 3.21 1.66 1.66 3.21 1.66 5.12 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 7.2 11.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.46em;--ltx-fo-height:7.79em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="110.54" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 107.85)" width="462.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS2.p6.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.46em;">
<span id="S4.SS2.p6.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RQ3 (Evaluation of LLM-based FOI <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. RQ3: Evaluation of LLM-based FOI ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§5.3</span></a>):<span id="S4.SS2.p6.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Is LLM-based FOI feasible? How well can LLMs craft valid meta drivers? Can these meta drivers uncover fresh coverage or detect new bugs?</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The ultimate goal of MFOE is to enhance existing fuzz drivers by leveraging oracles constructed with MRs.
The resulting meta drivers are expected to be stronger than their original version, uncovering additional coverage and exposing potential bugs.
RQ3 investigates this problem.
We first report statistics on the proportions of meta drivers that achieve the compilation-time and runtime validity, and then evaluate the fuzzing capabilities of the valid meta drivers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS2.p8.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="176.98" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 176.98" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,176.98) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.12 L 0 171.86 C 0 174.68 2.29 176.98 5.12 176.98 L 472.26 176.98 C 475.08 176.98 477.38 174.68 477.38 171.86 L 477.38 5.12 C 477.38 2.29 475.08 0 472.26 0 L 5.12 0 C 2.29 0 0 2.29 0 5.12 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.66 5.12 L 1.66 171.86 C 1.66 173.77 3.21 175.31 5.12 175.31 L 472.26 175.31 C 474.17 175.31 475.72 173.77 475.72 171.86 L 475.72 5.12 C 475.72 3.21 474.17 1.66 472.26 1.66 L 5.12 1.66 C 3.21 1.66 1.66 3.21 1.66 5.12 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 7.2 8.58)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.46em;--ltx-fo-height:11.55em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="159.82" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 159.82)" width="462.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS2.p8.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.46em;">
<span id="S4.SS2.p8.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p8.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RQ4 (LLM Sensitivity <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4. RQ4: LLM Sensitivity ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§5.4</span></a>):<span id="S4.SS2.p8.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Can MetaFOE work with different LLMs? How do different LLMs perform on the two tasks, i.e., MRG and FOI, of MetaFOE?</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The LLM is a core building block of MetaFOE and can influence the overall performance of MetaFOE.
RQ4 is designed to evaluate the sensitivity of MetaFOE to the concrete choice of LLM.
We aim to understand how different LLMs affect the feasibility and quality of the generated MRs and meta drivers, providing insights into the robustness and generality of the framework.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS2.p10.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="143.77" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 143.77" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,143.77) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.12 L 0 138.65 C 0 141.47 2.29 143.77 5.12 143.77 L 472.26 143.77 C 475.08 143.77 477.38 141.47 477.38 138.65 L 477.38 5.12 C 477.38 2.29 475.08 0 472.26 0 L 5.12 0 C 2.29 0 0 2.29 0 5.12 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.66 5.12 L 1.66 138.65 C 1.66 140.56 3.21 142.11 5.12 142.11 L 472.26 142.11 C 474.17 142.11 475.72 140.56 475.72 138.65 L 475.72 5.12 C 475.72 3.21 474.17 1.66 472.26 1.66 L 5.12 1.66 C 3.21 1.66 1.66 3.21 1.66 5.12 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 7.2 8.58)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.46em;--ltx-fo-height:9.15em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="126.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 126.61)" width="462.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS2.p10.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.46em;">
<span id="S4.SS2.p10.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p10.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">RQ5 (Prompt Sensitivity <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5. RQ5: Prompt Sensitivity ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§5.5</span></a>):<span id="S4.SS2.p10.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Can MetaFOE work with different prompt strategies? Will the choice of prompt strategy impacts the results of MRG and FOI?</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The widespread adoption of LLMs is attributed not only to their strong language processing capabilities, but also to the flexibility of prompting, which requires no extensive data collection or additional training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Previous studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> show that the choice of prompt strategy can significantly affect LLM performance in specific tasks, underscoring the need for a careful prompt design.
Since MetaFOE also prompts to tame LLMs, we design RQ5 to systematically investigate how MetaFOE performs with different prompt strategies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Fuzz Drivers</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All fuzz drivers used in our experiments are collected from OSS-Fuzz.
For RQ1, we consider all C/C++ projects and analyze the drivers from those that can be successfully compiled on our local experimental machine.
This process yields <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,068</span> drivers from <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">127</span> projects in total; details are described in <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1. RQ1: Existing Fuzz Oracles ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§5.1</span></a>.
For RQ2–RQ5, which systematically evaluate MetaFOE, we construct a curated subset by selecting 10 projects and choosing one representative driver for each project.
We then run MetaFOE with different LLMs and prompt strategies to generate MRs for the selected projects and implement <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">6,228</span> valid meta drivers.
Detailed statistics are presented in <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. RQ2: Evaluation of LLM-based MRG ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§5.2</span></a> and <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. RQ3: Evaluation of LLM-based FOI ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§5.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Large Language Models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we select <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">three</span> representative LLMs; their details are as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GLM-4-Plus</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhipu-AI, 2026</a>)</cite>.
GLM-4-Plus is a large language model developed by Zhipu AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
It is designed for general-purpose language understanding and generation tasks, including code generation, reasoning, and conversational assistance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">GLM et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Owing to its strong performance on code-related tasks, the GLM series has been widely adopted in both academic research and industrial applications, serving as a core model for software engineering tasks such as fuzzing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and software development <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-V3.2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek, 2026</a>)</cite>.
DeepSeek-V3.2 is a reasoning-oriented LLM developed by DeepSeek AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>; it has exhibited strong multilingual understanding and competitive performance in code generation and completion in multiple programming languages.
Given its effectiveness in program synthesis, bug detection, refactoring, and documentation generation, the DeepSeek-V3 series has drawn increasing attention in academia, advancing research in intelligent software engineering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4.1</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025b</a>)</cite>.
GPT-4.1 is a recent model in the GPT-4 family developed by OpenAI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025a</a>)</cite>. Recognized as one of the most influential LLM families, the GPT series has been widely adopted in both academia and industry, supporting a broad range of applications in software engineering and security <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sapia and Böhme, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
As the successor to GPT-4o, GPT-4.1 is more powerful and offers improved coding performance and long-context capabilities; we thus include it in our study.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">LLM Settings</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the model and the prompt strategy, temperature is another key factor that can significantly affect LLM outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
To ensure reproducible results while maintaining moderate output variability, we set the temperature to 0.2 for all studied LLMs in both MRG and FOI.
All other generation parameters are kept at their default values to avoid introducing additional tuning bias.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5. </span>Prompting Method</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first describe the prompt strategies adopted in this study and then present the specific prompting process designed for MRG and FOI.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.5.1. </span>Prompt Strategies</h4>

<div id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we study <span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">five</span> prompt strategies, namely zero-shot <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kojima et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, few-shot <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, crit ique <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, expert <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and chain-of-thought <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
We choose these prompt strategies because they are representative and widely adopted in fuzzing for driver generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, mutator synthesis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and input generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
These strategies are also applied to other software engineering tasks, such as metamorphic testing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, unit test generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and automatic program repair <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
The details of the studied prompt strategies are as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Zero-shot (<span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ZERO</span>).</span>
The zero-shot prompt directly instructs an LLM to perform a task without any task-specific examples, relying solely on its pre-trained knowledge.
In this study, we design <span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ZERO</span> as simple instructions to serve as a basic baseline, following the setup of prior studies that investigate LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Few-shot (<span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FEW</span>).</span>
In contrast to <span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ZERO</span>, which provides no task demonstrations in the instructions, the few-shot prompt provides a handful of examples to help the model better understand the task context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
The examples profile the expected input-output pairs, allowing LLMs to capture task intuitions more accurately.
We include <span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FEW</span> to investigate whether LLMs achieve better performance in MRG and FOI when provided with a small number of examples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Critique (<span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CRIT</span>).</span>
The critique prompt is designed to elicit evaluative feedback from an LLM; it asks the model to review, critique, or refine an existing generation by pointing out errors or suggesting improvements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
In this study, we employ <span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CRIT</span> prompts that explicitly point out errors in the LLM’s outputs to specific tasks, such as the inapplicable MRs produced in MRG and invalid meta drivers constructed in FOI.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expert (<span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXP</span>).</span>
The expert prompt instructs the LLM to assume the role of a domain expert, which biases its output toward specialized knowledge and style <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
In this study, we prepare the four other prompts with minimal expert-guided instructions, such as “<span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">you are a software testing expert familiar with metamorphic testing</span>” and “<span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">you are an expert in fuzz testing</span>”, to ensure that the LLM can handle MFOE tasks properly.
In addition to these basic instructions, we provide restrictive instructions to guide the LLM to focus more on specific tasks (i.e., MRG and FOI).
Specifically, we ask an LLM to (1) understand MRG or FOI by dissecting it into concrete steps and (2) generate a description of an expert competent for the given task from a second-person perspective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chain-of-thought (<span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span>).</span>
The chain-of-thought prompt encourages the LLM to explicitly articulate intermediate reasoning steps, helping the model better understand the task and thus improve its performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
To construct appropriate <span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span> templates for MRG and FOI, we summarize general workflows from prior studies on metamorphic testing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2018</a>)</cite> and fuzz driver generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Babić et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, and instantiate them as structured operation chains.
Details of the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span> prompts for MRG and FOI are provided in <a href="#S4.SS5.SSS2" title="4.5.2. Prompting for MRG ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§4.5.2</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS5.SSS3" title="4.5.3. Prompting for FOI ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§4.5.3</span></a>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.5.2. </span>Prompting for MRG</h4>

<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14164v1/prompt-mrg-inone.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/239;" aria-describedby="acmlabel4" width="476" height="239"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>. </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt templates used in MRG.</span><span id="acmlabel4" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">Prompt templates used in \techphaseA{}.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.5.2. Prompting for MRG ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 4</span></a>displays the prompt templates designed for MRG.
For clarity, we simplify these prompts by omitting non-critical content.
The text segments formatted as <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#C55A11;">[% var %]</span> are prompt variables that will be instantiated during prompt construction.
For example, the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#C55A11;">[% project %]</span> field will be replaced with concrete information (e.g., documentation and source code) of the library under test, guiding the LLM to generate MRs for the library.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.5.2. Prompting for MRG ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 4</span></a>(a) presents the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ZERO</span> template, which also serves as the prompt skeleton.
When constructing prompts for <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FEW</span>, <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CRIT</span>, <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXP</span>, and <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span>, the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#C55A11;">[% prompt %]</span> field in this skeleton is replaced with the extended content shown in <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.5.2. Prompting for MRG ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 4</span></a>(b)–(e).
In addition to the variables, the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ZERO</span> prompt consists of three components:
(1) <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Basic role settings</span>, which specify the fundamental expertise and task goals of the LLM.
(2) <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Output constraints</span>, which instruct the LLM to generate 10 distinct MRs.
(3) <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Output formatting</span>, which stipulates the structure of the generated MRs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.5.2. Prompting for MRG ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 4</span></a>(b) presents the additional prompt for <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FEW</span>, where two examples are provided to help the LLM understand the goal of MRG and illustrate the expected format of the generated MRs.
To construct these examples, we first manually analyze two real-world projects, TinyXML2 and LibPNG, and extract one representative MR from each; the extracted MRs are then organized according to the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ZERO</span> template.
Both projects are integrated in OSS-Fuzz and are widely used in fuzzing evaluation, making them suitable examples for MFOE.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.5.2. Prompting for MRG ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 4</span></a>(c) presents the additional prompt for <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CRIT</span>, where the LLM is instructed to review the previously generated MRs from three aspects: applicability, bug-detection potential, and oracle clarity.
The detailed descriptions of these aspects are provided immediately afterward to facilitate the LLM’s understanding.
Through the self-critique process, we aim for the generated MRs to be not only applicable to the target library but also testable and easy to validate by humans.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.5.2. Prompting for MRG ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 4</span></a>(d) presents the additional prompt for <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXP</span>, which comprises three parts:
(1) <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Expert role specification</span>, which further specifies the LLM expertise beyond the basic ones provided in the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ZERO</span> template.
(2) <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Expert capability justification</span>, which defines criteria for high-quality MRs and facilitates the reasoning process.
(3) <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Expert task principles</span>, which decomposes and constrains the MRG task from the expert perspective.
Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we construct the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXP</span> prompt with the assistance of an LLM via few-shot prompting.
Specifically, we adopt the task description for code summarization provided by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2025)</a></cite> as an example, and supply the key steps of MRG (e.g., understanding the provided documentation and extracting I/O relations) to prompt ChatGPT (powered by GPT-4o) to synthesize the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXP</span> template.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.5.2. Prompting for MRG ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 4</span></a>(e) presents the additional prompt for <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span>, where MRG is divided into three steps.
First, we instruct the LLM to identify and focus on sentences that describe the input–output (IO) relations of the target libraries; these IO relations are essential for effective metamorphic testing, as they guide the generation of follow-up test cases and underpin the validation of MRs (described in <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2. Metamorphic Testing ‣ 2. Background ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§2.2</span></a>).
Second, we instruct the LLM to filter out IO relations that are <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> suitable for defining MRs.
For example, in an XML parsing project such as TinyXML2, inserting comments into a document will not change its parsed structure, which constitutes a suitable IO relation to define an MR.
In contrast, changing the order of attributes within an XML element should not change the element’s semantics, rendering it seemly an appropriate IO relation for defining an MR; however, this relation may lead to different serialized outputs, which makes it difficult to validate reliably and thus unsuitable for MR construction.
Third, for the remaining IO relations, we instruct the LLM to convert them into MRs according to the format defined in the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ZERO</span> template.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.5.3. </span>Prompting for FOI</h4>

<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14164v1/prompt-foi-inone.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/239;" aria-describedby="acmlabel5" width="476" height="239"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>. </span><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt templates used in FOI.</span><span id="acmlabel5" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">Prompt templates used in \techphaseB{}.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.5.3. Prompting for FOI ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 5</span></a>displays the prompt templates for FOI.
Similarly to <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.5.2. Prompting for MRG ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 4</span></a>, <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.5.3. Prompting for FOI ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 5</span></a>(a) presents the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ZERO</span> template (the prompt skeleton), and <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.5.3. Prompting for FOI ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 5</span></a>(b)–<a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.5.3. Prompting for FOI ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 5</span></a>(e) present the additional prompts for specific strategies.
As shown in <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.5.3. Prompting for FOI ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 5</span></a>(a), FOI takes two input variables: (1) the original driver to be enhanced (i.e., <span id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#C55A11;">[% driver %]</span>) and (2) the MR to be instantiated as a fuzz oracle (i.e., <span id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#C55A11;">[% MR %]</span>).
After setting the basic role of the LLM as a fuzzing expert and specifying the input variables, the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ZERO</span> template further constrains the output format and instructs the LLM to output only code.
The optional prompt below the dashed line is for compilation-level driver validation (described in <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS2" title="4.1.2. Fuzz Oracle Implementation (FOI) ‣ 4.1. MetaFOE ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§4.1.2</span></a>); it instructs the LLM to resolve the compilation issues of the meta driver candidates using the error messages as guidance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.5.3. Prompting for FOI ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 5</span></a>(b), the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FEW</span> prompt includes an example that demonstrates how to implement and incorporate a metamorphic-based oracle into a given driver.
The example comprises three parts: (1) the original fuzz driver, (2) the MR to be implemented, and (3) the modified fuzz driver equipped with the new metamorphic oracle, which is implemented as an <span id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert</span>.
We craft this example by manually implementing the MR “<span id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Adding an attribute to XML element should increase the attribute count of the element by 1</span>” in a driver from the TinyXML2 project.
Details of the prompt can be found in our artifact <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">MetaFOE-Team, 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.5.3. Prompting for FOI ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 5</span></a>(c), the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CRIT</span> prompt first instructs the LLM to review its previous output to ensure that (1) the given MR is correctly implemented, (2) the modified driver remains consistent with its original structure, and (3) the modification does <span id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> introduce false positives or unnecessary executions.
Based on the review results, the prompt further instructs the LLM to revise its output and re-generate the modified fuzz drivers if any of the above checks are violated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.5.3. Prompting for FOI ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 5</span></a>(d), the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXP</span> prompt further strengthens the LLM’s capability to perform FOI by (1) specifying stronger expertise in fuzzing and metamorphic testing, (2) highlighting key considerations in implementing metamorphic-based oracles for fuzzing, and (3) concretizing the criteria of accomplishing FOI with high quality.
Consistent with MRG, the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXP</span> prompt for FOI is likewise synthesized with the assistance of ChatGPT via few-shot prompting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.5.3. Prompting for FOI ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 5</span></a>(e), the <span id="S4.SS5.SSS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span> prompt instructs the LLM to perform FOI in four steps.
First, analyze the given MR to understand its intention.
Second, identify the constraints intended by the MR, which define how outputs change in response to input transformations.
Third, determine how to implement the identified constraints within the given driver’s context and plan the modification accordingly.
Fourth, trigger the generation of the meta driver.
Collectively, these four steps are designed based on the general workflows of fuzz driver generation and metamorphic testing (see <a href="#S2" title="2. Background ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure" aria-describedby="acmlabel6">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F6.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:169.1pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14164v1/driver-example-abort.svg" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:234/156;" width="234" height="156"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F6.fig1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
An Abort oracle in driver <span id="S4.F6.fig1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">entropy_fuzz</span> from project Htslib <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Htslib, 2026</a>)</cite>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F6.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:169.1pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14164v1/driver-example-san.svg" id="S4.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:234/156;" width="234" height="156"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F6.fig2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
A Sanitizer oracle in driver <span id="S4.F6.fig2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fuzz_asn</span> from project Krb5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krb5, 2026</a>)</cite>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>. </span><span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Example drivers implementing Abort and Sanitizer oracles.</span><span id="acmlabel6" class="ltx_nodisplay ltx_acm_description">Examples of Abort and Sanitizer Oracles</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents the study results, which are organized by the sequence of RQs (<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Research Questions ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§4.2</span></a>).
For each RQ, we first summarize the key findings and then present the detailed results.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Experimental Setup for MFOE</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our study comprises two group of experiments: running MetaFOE (i.e., MRG and FOI) and fuzzing with meta drivers.
All experiments are conducted on a server equipped with an Intel(R) Xeon(R) Silver 4316 CPU @ 2.30GHz (80 logical cores) and 512 GB of RAM, running Ubuntu 20.04.6 LTS.
In total, MetaFOE consumes <span id="S5.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">213 M</span> tokens.
For fuzzing, we run each driver to for <span id="S5.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">three</span> hours.
For projects that prepare seed sets in the original build scripts, we directly use these seeds as initial seeds.
For the remaining projects that do not explicitly provide initial seeds, we collect seeds by crawling the internet or from the dataset provided by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Herrera et al. (2021)</a></cite>.
All initial seeds are available in our artifact <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">MetaFOE-Team, 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span><span id="S5.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1:</span> Existing Fuzz Oracles</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS1.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="192.81" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 192.81" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,192.81) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.12 L 0 187.69 C 0 190.52 2.29 192.81 5.12 192.81 L 472.26 192.81 C 475.08 192.81 477.38 190.52 477.38 187.69 L 477.38 5.12 C 477.38 2.29 475.08 0 472.26 0 L 5.12 0 C 2.29 0 0 2.29 0 5.12 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#ECECEC;" fill="#ECECEC" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.66 5.12 L 1.66 187.69 C 1.66 189.6 3.21 191.15 5.12 191.15 L 472.26 191.15 C 474.17 191.15 475.72 189.6 475.72 187.69 L 475.72 5.12 C 475.72 3.21 474.17 1.66 472.26 1.66 L 5.12 1.66 C 3.21 1.66 1.66 3.21 1.66 5.12 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 7.2 8.58)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.46em;--ltx-fo-height:12.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="175.66" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 175.66)" width="462.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS1.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.46em;">
<span id="S5.SS1.p1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Finding 1:<span id="S5.SS1.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Existing fuzz oracles can be classified into four types, namely None, Assert, Abort, and Sanitizer.
Among the 1,068 drivers that we collect from OSS-Fuzz, the four oracle types account for 63.9%, 18.3%, 16.1%, and 1.8%, respectively.
Considering both the types and purposes, 81.1% of the existing fuzz drivers do not implement any functional oracles to verify the APIs under test.
These observations highlight the critical need for (automatic) fuzz oracle enhancement.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1. </span>Collection of Fuzz Drivers</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We collect fuzz drivers from OSS-Fuzz in three steps.
First, we implement scripts to download and build OSS-Fuzz projects on a local machine.
Second, based on the build results, we identify driver source files and record driver names with another Python script.
Through these two steps, we obtain <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,078</span> plausible drivers.
Third, we manually inspect drivers’ source code to exclude those that appear to be drivers but are actually not (e.g., the AFL glue code <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">afl_driver.c</span>).
As a result, we filter out 10 fake drivers and finally obtain <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,068</span> drivers from <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">127</span> projects.
All drivers and scripts can be found in our artifact <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">MetaFOE-Team, 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2. </span>Analysis of Existing Fuzz Oracles</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the source code of the collected drivers, we classify the oracles implemented in these drivers into four types.
The details are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">None Oracle</span>.
A fuzz driver is classified as implementing a None oracle if it contains no dedicated bug-detection mechanisms and relies solely on program crashes (e.g., memory corruptions) to indicate bugs.
An example of a None oracle is displayed in <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Fuzz Driver ‣ 2.1. Greybox Fuzzing ‣ 2. Background ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 1</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assert Oracle</span>.
A fuzz driver is classified as implementing an Assert oracle if it invokes standard <span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert</span> statements or custom assertion mechanisms.
An example driver with an Assertion oracle is shown in <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Fuzz Driver ‣ 2.1. Greybox Fuzzing ‣ 2. Background ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 1</span></a>, which includes two standard <span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert</span> statements.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Abort Oracle</span>.
A fuzz driver is classified as implementing an Abort oracle if it raises abnormal exits.
Given that most of the OSS-Fuzz drivers are LibFuzzer-style, we treat invocations of <span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abort</span>, <span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">__builtin_trap</span>, and <span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">exit</span> (with non-zero exit code) statements as abnormal exits, as LibFuzzer leverages these statements to signal crashing or failing inputs.
<a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.5.3. Prompting for FOI ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 6</span></a> illustrates an example of an Abort oracle, where the driver invokes <span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abort</span> (colored blue) when the values of certain variables are not as expected.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sanitizer Oracle</span>.
A fuzz driver implements mechanisms to facilitate sanitizers (e.g., ASAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clang-LLVM, 2026a</a>)</cite> and MSAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clang-LLVM, 2026b</a>)</cite>) in detecting erroneous behaviors.
<a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.5.3. Prompting for FOI ‣ 4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 6</span></a> illustrates an example of a Sanitizer oracle, where the driver first defines a macro <span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FUZZ_ASAN</span> to assist ASAN in detecting bugs by triggering carefully crafted memory manipulations, and then invokes this macro multiple times in the driver body.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5.1.2. Analysis of Existing Fuzz Oracles ‣ 5.1. RQ1: Existing Fuzz Oracles ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 7</span></a>shows the proportions of different types of fuzz oracles.
Among the <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,068</span> OSS-Fuzz drivers that we collect, 63.9% (682) implement <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">no</span> explicit oracles (i.e., None type) and rely on crashes to indicate bugs.
This observation aligns with common practice in modern fuzzing, where crashes are often used as implicit oracles.
In addition to the None type, 18.3% (195) of the drivers implement Assert oracles by invoking at least one <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert</span> statement, and 16.3% (172) implement Abort oracles.
The Sanitizer oracles account for 1.8% (19), which is the lowest among the four types of oracles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that although some drivers implement Assert or Abort oracles, these oracles may not be intended to verify the tested APIs.
Inspired by this observation, we further inspect the source code of the 367 drivers that implement Assert and Abort oracles to identify the exact purposes.
Two types of oracle purposes are observed:
(1) <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Result verification</span>, for which the oracle checks the return values of tested APIs and alarms when the values are not as expected.
(2) <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Valid execution</span>, for which the oracle enforces certain preconditions (e.g., a previous API call succeeds and returns an expected value) to ensure that the driver is executed with valid states and inputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5.1.2. Analysis of Existing Fuzz Oracles ‣ 5.1. RQ1: Existing Fuzz Oracles ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 7</span></a>visualizes the proportion of purposes among the Assert and Abort oracles, where we observe that 50.1% (184) of the drivers implement oracles only for valid execution; this observation confirms that only a few drivers implement functional oracles.
To quantify the proportion of functional fuzz oracles, we label Sanitizer oracles and Assert/Abort oracles used for result verification as functional, and treat the remaining ones as non-functional.
As shown in <a href="#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5.1.2. Analysis of Existing Fuzz Oracles ‣ 5.1. RQ1: Existing Fuzz Oracles ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 7</span></a>, only 18.9% of the <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,068</span> drivers implement functional oracles, underscoring the urgent need to improve fuzz oracles.
Given that metamorphic-based oracles can provide strong support for functional verification of the APIs under test, these observations highlight the potential of MFOE and motivate our study.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F7" class="ltx_figure" aria-describedby="acmlabel7">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F7.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:112.1pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14164v1/props-oracle-types.svg" id="S5.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:155/155;" width="155" height="155"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F7.fig1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F7.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Distribution of None, Assert, Abort, and Sanitizer oracles.
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F7.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:112.1pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14164v1/props-oracle-purposes.svg" id="S5.F7.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:155/153;" width="155" height="153"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F7.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F7.fig2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
