<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span><span id="S5.SS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3:</span> Evaluation of LLM-based FOI</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS3.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="444.57" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 444.57" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,444.57) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.12 L 0 439.45 C 0 442.28 2.29 444.57 5.12 444.57 L 472.26 444.57 C 475.08 444.57 477.38 442.28 477.38 439.45 L 477.38 5.12 C 477.38 2.29 475.08 0 472.26 0 L 5.12 0 C 2.29 0 0 2.29 0 5.12 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#ECECEC;" fill="#ECECEC" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.66 5.12 L 1.66 439.45 C 1.66 441.36 3.21 442.91 5.12 442.91 L 472.26 442.91 C 474.17 442.91 475.72 441.36 475.72 439.45 L 475.72 5.12 C 475.72 3.21 474.17 1.66 472.26 1.66 L 5.12 1.66 C 3.21 1.66 1.66 3.21 1.66 5.12 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 7.2 11.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.46em;--ltx-fo-height:30.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="427.41" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 424.72)" width="462.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS3.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.46em;">
<span id="S5.SS3.p1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Finding 3:<span id="S5.SS3.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Based on the generated MRs, MetaFOE implements 830–1,680 meta driver candidates for the 10 subjects, among which 6,228 (50.4%) are valid in total.
Extensive fuzzing with groups of three-hour campaigns show that:
(1) On average, the meta drivers cover 18.7% more edges than the original drivers by encompassing 38.0 more LoC and 4.1 more API calls.
(2) 30–508 unique crashes are triggered by the meta drivers on the 10 projects, eight of which the original drivers fail to detect any crashes.
These results indicate that metamorphic-based oracles can improve both edge coverage and crash discovery.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.1. </span>FOI Statistics</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The columns 7–9 of <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.2.2. MRG Statistics ‣ 5.2. RQ2: Evaluation of LLM-based MRG ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a> exhibit the FOI results, where #D, #CD, and #VD denote the numbers of implemented, compilable, and valid drivers.
Similarly, we present the valid proportion (VP) in the tiny font, which is calculated as <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\text{\#VD}}{\text{\#D}}\times 100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mtext>#VD</mtext><mtext>#D</mtext></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\text{\#VD}}{\text{\#D}}\times 100\%</annotation></semantics></math>.
Based on applicable MRs, MetaFOE implements metamorphic-based oracles and crafts <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">830–1,680</span> meta drivers.
The VPs of the implemented meta drivers range from <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">26.1% to 78.1%</span> among subjects, with the lowest VP observed in Libyaml and the highest in Zlib.
The overall VP is <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">50.4%</span>, indicating that about half of the meta drivers are invalid.
Losses of meta driver candidates are observed in both validation stages of FOI (described in <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS2" title="4.1.2. Fuzz Oracle Implementation (FOI) ‣ 4.1. MetaFOE ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§4.1.2</span></a>), demonstrating the necessity of the two-stage design.
Specifically, <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">14.1%</span> of the candidates are discarded by compilation-level validation, indicating that generating compilable meta drivers remains a challenge for LLMs.
A much larger proportion (<span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">85.9%</span>) of candidates are discarded by runtime-level validation, suggesting that LLMs have limited ability to predict runtime behaviors of generated drivers.
Among those discarded during runtime-level validation, <span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">98.7%</span> are removed because they trigger excessive crashes, while the rest are discarded for achieving too little coverage.
These results indicate that LLMs tend to implement metamorphic-based oracles that induce false positives, reflecting the limitations of modern LLMs in FOI.</p>
</div>
