<section id="S5.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.3. </span>Crash Exposure</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The lower subtable of <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3.2. Code Coverage ‣ 5.3. RQ3: Evaluation of LLM-based FOI ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a> reports the average number of crashes triggered by the original and all valid meta drivers.
As shown, only the original drivers of S01 and S07 expose crashes during <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">three</span>-hour fuzzing, whereas the meta drivers find crashes in all projects.
Specifically, meta drivers trigger <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1.3–70.6</span> crashes on the 10 subecjts, achieving <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">11.9<math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> and 1.9<math id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></span> of crashes than the original drivers in S01 and S07, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We further analyze the crashes to remove duplicates and identify their types.
To this end, we tag the first line of the crash stack as a summary for each crash and deduplicate crashes based on these summaries.
Two types of crashes during this process:
(1) <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory Corruption (MC)</span>.
We follow the default settings of OSS-Fuzz and compile the drivers with sanitizers (i.e., ASAN, MSAN, and UBSAN), which are designed to cause crashes when suspicious memory operations are detected.
(2) <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assertion Failure (AF)</span>.
We are particularly interested in this type of crash because MetaFOE is devised to implement metamorphic-based oracles using <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The results of the crash analysis are shown in the rightmost four columns of <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.3.1. FOI Statistics ‣ 5.3. RQ3: Evaluation of LLM-based FOI ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 2</span></a>.
The original drivers only triggered <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">two</span> crashes in total, where <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">one MC</span> from S07 and <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">one AF</span> from S01.
For meta drivers, <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">30–508</span> unique crashes are detected during <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">three</span>-hour fuzzing, of which <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">62</span> are MCs and <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,466</span> AFs.
These results suggest that metamorphic-based oracles can significantly improve crash detection, especially for AFs, as MetaFOE implements such oracles using <span id="S5.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert</span>.
However, it is worth noting that meta drivers may introduce false positives and require additional diagnosis and triage, as also observed in prior metamorphic testing approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Le et al., 2014</a>)</cite>.
We further discuss false positive identification and mitigation in <a href="#S6.SS1" title="6.1. Case Study ‣ 6. Discussion ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§6.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
