<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS4.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="361.55" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 361.55" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,361.55) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.12 L 0 356.43 C 0 359.25 2.29 361.55 5.12 361.55 L 472.26 361.55 C 475.08 361.55 477.38 359.25 477.38 356.43 L 477.38 5.12 C 477.38 2.29 475.08 0 472.26 0 L 5.12 0 C 2.29 0 0 2.29 0 5.12 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#ECECEC;" fill="#ECECEC" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.66 5.12 L 1.66 356.43 C 1.66 358.34 3.21 359.88 5.12 359.88 L 472.26 359.88 C 474.17 359.88 475.72 358.34 475.72 356.43 L 475.72 5.12 C 475.72 3.21 474.17 1.66 472.26 1.66 L 5.12 1.66 C 3.21 1.66 1.66 3.21 1.66 5.12 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 7.2 11.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.46em;--ltx-fo-height:24.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="344.39" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 341.7)" width="462.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS4.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.46em;">
<span id="S5.SS4.p1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Finding 4:<span id="S5.SS4.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The performance of MetaFOE is sensitive to the choice of LLMs in both MRG and FOI.
DeepSeek-V3.2 performs best, achieving the highest MR count (1,246) and applicability (82.9%) in MRG, and showing competitive performance with GPT-4.1 in FOI.
In contrast, GLM-4-Plus performs worst, with the lowest applicability (71.1%) and valid meta drivers (41.1%).
Considering the release dates, newer and more capable LLMs may lead to better performance.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.1. </span>Overview of Results</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.4. RQ4: LLM Sensitivity ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a> presents the MRG and FOI results under different configurations, with different LLMs shown in the columns and different prompt strategies in the rows.
The upper subtable displays the statistics of generated MRs, whereas the lower subtable displays FOI results.
The Total columns report the sums of prompt strategies, while the Tot. row reports the sums by LLMs.
The #DR row reports the number of distinct MRs gathered by each LLM; we calculate this number by aggregating all MRs generated by the same LLM and then deduplicating them.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.2. </span>MRG Results across LLMs</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We omit the version and use GLM, DeepSeek, and GPT to denote the studied LLMs (see <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. Large Language Models ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§4.4</span></a>) for clarity.
As shown in <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.4. RQ4: LLM Sensitivity ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a>, GLM, DeepSeek, and GPT generate <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,149</span>, <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,246</span>, and <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,080</span> MRs, of which <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">71.1%</span>, <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">82.9%</span>, <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">77.3%</span> are applicable.
The performance of MetaFOE in MRG varies significantly across LLMs in terms of both the number of generated MRs and the AP.
Specifically, as the most recent model considered in our study, DeepSeek performs the best among the studied LLMs, generating <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">216 (+11.8%)</span> and <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">198 (+5.6%)</span> more applicable MRs than GLM and GPT.
GLM and GPT produce similar numbers of distinct MRs (i.e., <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">735 and 751</span>), whereas DeepSeek again performs the best, generating about <span id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">one third</span> more distinct MRs.
These results indicate that the effectiveness of MRG is sensitive to the choice of LLM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.3. </span>FOI Results across LLMs</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We instruct each LLM to implement meta drivers based on the distinct MRs it produced.
The purpose of this experimental setup is to evaluate each LLM’s ability to handle the MFOE task independently.
In practice, the MRs generated by an LLM are general and can be used by another LLM as well.
As shown in <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.4. RQ4: LLM Sensitivity ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a>, GLM, DeepSeek, GPT implement <span id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">3,636, 4,974, and 3,741</span> meta drivers, of which <span id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,493, 2,663, and 2,072</span> are valid.
GLM performs the worst among the three models, with a VP of only <span id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">41.1%</span>; it cannot even stably generate meta driver candidates in a quantity that exactly matches the number of distinct MRs (<span id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">735</span>), instead producing <span id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">713–737</span> meta driver candidates.
GPT achieves the best VP, with <span id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">55.4%</span> of meta driver candidates being valid.
Although DeepSeek implements the most valid meta drivers (<span id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">2,663</span>), this can be attributed to its largest number of input MRs (<span id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">995</span>), and its VP is <span id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1.9%</span> lower than that of GPT.
These results indicate that the effectiveness of FOI is also highly dependent on the choice of LLM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The columns <span id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">4–6</span> of <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3.2. Code Coverage ‣ 5.3. RQ3: Evaluation of LLM-based FOI ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a> report the fuzzing results achieved by the meta drivers implemented using different LLMs, where the better results are colored in darker blue.
As shown, the meta drivers implemented by GPT achieve the best performance in terms of code coverage, obtaining the highest average edge coverage on <span id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">nine</span> out of 10 projects.
Regarding triggered crashes, DeepSeek performs the best, as its meta drivers trigger the highest average number of crashes on <span id="S5.SS4.SSS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">six</span> projects.
Overall, these results indicate that DeepSeek and GPT achieve comparable performance, each showing advantages in generating meta drivers that excel in crash detection and code coverage.
Moreover, these findings confirm that MetaFOE is sensitive to the choice of LLMs in the FOI phase.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5. </span><span id="S5.SS5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ5:</span> Prompt Sensitivity</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS5.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="378.15" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 378.15" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,378.15) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.12 L 0 373.03 C 0 375.86 2.29 378.15 5.12 378.15 L 472.26 378.15 C 475.08 378.15 477.38 375.86 477.38 373.03 L 477.38 5.12 C 477.38 2.29 475.08 0 472.26 0 L 5.12 0 C 2.29 0 0 2.29 0 5.12 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#ECECEC;" fill="#ECECEC" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.66 5.12 L 1.66 373.03 C 1.66 374.94 3.21 376.49 5.12 376.49 L 472.26 376.49 C 474.17 376.49 475.72 374.94 475.72 373.03 L 475.72 5.12 C 475.72 3.21 474.17 1.66 472.26 1.66 L 5.12 1.66 C 3.21 1.66 1.66 3.21 1.66 5.12 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 7.2 11.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.46em;--ltx-fo-height:25.89em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="361" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 358.31)" width="462.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS5.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.46em;">
<span id="S5.SS5.p1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Finding 5:<span id="S5.SS5.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Compared with the sensitivity to LLM choices, MetaFOE is less sensitive to the choice of prompt strategies.
All strategies perform closely in MRG, with a standard deviation of 0.7%.
For FOI, <span id="S5.SS5.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span> performs the best by implementing the largest number of valid meta drivers (1,389) with the highest valid proportion (56.2%), whereas <span id="S5.SS5.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXP</span> performs the worst.
Regarding average fuzzing capabilities, <span id="S5.SS5.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXP</span> generates meta drivers that achieve the best results, where <span id="S5.SS5.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span> performs the worst in both edge coverage and crash detection.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.5.1. </span>MRG Results across Prompts.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rows of <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.4. RQ4: LLM Sensitivity ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a> present the results of MetaFOE under different prompt strategies, where the totals are reported in the last three columns.
As shown, MetaFOE achieves similar performance across different prompt strategies in terms of AP, with a standard deviation of only <span id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">0.7%</span>; and <span id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span> achieves the highest AP (78.1%).
Despite the similar AP values, <span id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span> generates the largest number of MRs (<span id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">856</span>), while the other four strategies generate comparable numbers of applicable MRs (<span id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">453–464</span>).
This is because <span id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span> is configured to generate MRs based on the provided project information and is not constrained by a predefined number, while the other four strategies are instructed to generate <span id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">20</span> in total (<span id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">10</span> each time).
We adopt this configuration because these four strategies tend to produce duplicates after generating around <span id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">20</span> MRs, while <span id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span> does not exhibit this issue.
Overall, <span id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span> performs the best among the five studied prompt strategies in MRG, and MetaFOE is largely insensitive to the choice of prompt strategies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.5.2. </span>FOI Results across Prompts.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in the lower subtable of <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.4. RQ4: LLM Sensitivity ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a>, MetaFOE achieves VP values ranging from <span id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">47.2%–56.2%</span> across the five prompt strategies, with <span id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span> performing the best and <span id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXP</span> the worst.
The columns <span id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">7–11</span> of <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3.2. Code Coverage ‣ 5.3. RQ3: Evaluation of LLM-based FOI ‣ 5. Results ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a> present the fuzzing results achieved under different prompt strategies, where the larger number of edges (crashes) is indicated in darker green.
As shown, the meta drivers implemented under different prompt strategies exhibit similar coverage, with <span id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXP</span> performing the best with slight overall advantages.
For crash detection, <span id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXP</span> also performs the best, generating meta drivers that trigger the highest average number of crashes on <span id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">six</span> projects.
Surprisingly, <span id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span> performs the worst in both edge coverage and triggered crashes, despite its superior generation statistics in terms of MRs and meta drivers.
This implies that <span id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span> is effective in accomplishing the tasks of MFOE, but cannot guarantee the fuzzing capabilities of meta drivers.
A mixed prompting strategy (e.g., <span id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span>+<span id="S5.SS5.SSS2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXP</span>) may yield better overall results.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents (1) a case study on crash analysis and LLM-assisted false-positive mitigation, (2) lessons learned, and (3) threats to validity.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>Case Study</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S6.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 6.1. Case Study ‣ 6. Discussion ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 8</span></a> illustrates a meta driver constructed in Libyaml (S06).
The original driver is shown in the subfig (a), the implemented MR (MR-021) in (b), and the core MR-related code in the meta driver in (c).
As shown, the original driver implements a basic test sequence, which initializes the test input, invokes the core API under test (<span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">yaml_parse_initialize</span>), and releases resources after fuzzing.
<span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MR-021</span> captures a whitespace-invariance property: appending trailing whitespace to a YAML document should not change its parsing results, i.e., both the event sequence and resulting data structures remain identical.
The meta driver incorporates <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MR-021</span> by appending trailing whitespace (i.e., three spaces, two tabs, and four newlines) to the input, re-parsing it, and verifying the equivalence of structured event sequences between the original and transformed executions.
The core function of the meta driver is <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">collect_events</span>, which parses a given YAML input and collects the resulting sequence of parsing events into a structured array for subsequent comparison; the source code is shown in <a href="#S6.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 6.1. Case Study ‣ 6. Discussion ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 8</span></a>(d), with the MR-supporting code in blue.
At first glance, MetaFOE correctly implements the meta driver, with a valid MR and functional metamorphic testing logic.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.14164v1/case-study-inone.svg" id="S6.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/425;" width="476" height="425" aria-label="Case study"><p>Case study</p></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>. </span><span id="S6.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">A meta driver implemented in Libyaml (S06).</span><span id="acmlabel8" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">Case study</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.1.1. </span>Meta-driver-induced False Positives</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Through <span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">three</span>-hour fuzzing, the meta driver shown in <a href="#S6.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 6.1. Case Study ‣ 6. Discussion ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 8</span></a> triggers 141 crashes in total, all of which are assertion failures.
After deduplication based on crash summaries, we observe that all crashes in fact originate from a single unique assertion failure (AF), which corresponds to a violation of the <span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">scalar_value</span> check enforced by the <span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert</span> statement highlighted in red.
We further analyze this AF by examining the YAML specification and consulting ChatGPT, and conclude that it is a false positive.
The failure stems from an invalid MR assumption rather than a Libyaml bug.
Appending trailing whitespace can alter the parsed content of block scalars (e.g., “<span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">|</span>” and “<span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&gt;</span>’’) without a terminating newline<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                
                
                
              YAML specification (block): <a href="https://yaml.org/spec/1.2.1/#Block" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://yaml.org/spec/1.2.1/#Block</a></span></span></span>
, under which <span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MR-021</span> does not hold, leading to false positives.
This observation indicates that, although LLMs are trained on large corpora and are capable of code understanding and test generation, they still cannot cover all corner cases and may therefore fail to implement fully correct metamorphic-based oracles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.1.2. </span>Mitigating False Positives</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We explore mitigating false positives with the help of ChatGPT.
To this end, we ask ChatGPT to refine the meta driver and provide it with three inputs: (1) the meta driver shown along with <span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MR-021</span>, (2) the root cause description of the false positive (i.e., how the MR may not hold for YAML block scalars), and (3) a example crashing input generated during fuzzing.
ChatGPT returns a patching plan that restricts the transformation to inputs ending with “<span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">\n</span>”, and generates a refined meta driver.
This ensures proper YAML termination, preventing appended whitespace from affecting scalar content and allowing <span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MR-021</span> to hold for the <span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert</span> highlighted in blue.
The refined patch is shown in the upper-right corner of <a href="#S6.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 6.1. Case Study ‣ 6. Discussion ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig. 8</span></a>(c).
A three-hour fuzzing of the refined meta driver still triggers <span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">162</span> crashes, all of which originate from the blue-highlighted <span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">assert</span> statement.
This result is unexpected and motivates a deeper inspection of the generated inputs.
We observe that, although false positives persist, the crashing inputs no longer exhibit the same pattern (i.e., ending with “<span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&gt;</span>” or “<span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">|</span>”) as those from the original meta driver, indicating that the patch is effective.
This suggests that the false positives are only partially mitigated, with the addressed cases largely corresponding to the provided example inputs (i.e., those ending with “<span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&gt;</span>” or “<span id="S6.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">|</span>”).
Overall, while LLM-based refinement can mitigate false positives, it may require multiple iterations with diverse crashing inputs. We leave this for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>Lessons Learned</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize the following lessons that may be helpful to the fuzzing community.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Fuzz Drivers Need Functional Oracles.</span>
Our analysis of <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,068</span> shows that 81.1% of OSS-Fuzz drivers lack functional oracles. Unlike implicit crash oracles, functional oracles require a deep understanding of the target library and are difficult to implement, especially for non-vendor users.
We thus call for better oracle design from vendors and highlight the need for automated techniques (such as our approach MetaFOE) to support fuzz oracle construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) LLMs are Capable in MFOE.</span>
Our experiments with <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">three</span> LLMs and <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">five</span> prompt strategies demonstrate that LLMs are feasible for realizing MFOE.
Better performance can be achieved with more capable models (e.g., DeepSeek-V3.2) and mixed prompt strategies (e.g., <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">COT</span>+<span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">EXP</span>), as different prompts excel at different aspects.
We hope these findings can inform future research.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) LLM-generated Meta Drivers Need Refinement.</span>
Although LLMs are capable of generating applicable MRs and valid meta drivers, the resulting meta drivers may still suffer from false positives even after multi-stage validation.
Our case study shows that LLMs can provide refinement patches to mitigate such issues, suggesting the need for an additional refinement stage to iteratively improve the quality of generated meta drivers.
We leave this to future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3. </span>Threats to Validity</h3>

<section id="S6.SS3.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Internal Validity</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Internal validity correlates with the completeness and fairness of the methodology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ampatzoglou et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Görz et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
In our study, threats to internal validity mainly arise from the generation and evaluation of MRs and modified drivers.
We address these threats by (1) constructing MFOE workflows based on established studies in metamorphic testing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, fuzz driver generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, and LLM for software engineering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and by (2) adopting and refining evaluation metrics from these pioneering works.
These efforts help to ensure internal validity to a large extent.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">External Validity</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">External validity refers to the extent to which the results can be generalized to subjects beyond those included in the study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ampatzoglou et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Görz et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
In this paper, we (1) investigate various fuzz drivers from OSS-Fuzz and (2) include multiple LLMs (<a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. Large Language Models ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§4.4</span></a>) and prompting strategies (<a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. Prompting Method ‣ 4. Study Design ‣ Investigating Metamorphic Fuzz Oracle Enhancement via Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§4.5</span></a>).
We also perform fuzzing for <span id="S6.SS3.SSSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">18,684</span> CPU hours to evaluate the capabilities of the valid meta drivers.
These efforts strengthen the external validity.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S7.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Fuzz Driver Generation</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fuzz drivers are essential components of fuzzing and a growing body of work has been devoted to its automatic generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lyu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sherman and Nagy, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Recent studies continue to tackle the open challenges of automatic driver generation; examples include WildSync <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and LibErator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Toffalini et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
With the advancement of modern LLMs, intelligent driver generation techniques have also emerged <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
In contrast to prior work that focuses on synthesizing call sequences and expanding API coverage, our study shifts attention to oracles, the critical components responsible for bug detection.
Note that although <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sherman and Nagy (2025)</a></cite> propose an oracle-guided technique, the “oracle” in there refers to evaluation criteria for driver quality rather than the bug-detection mechanism, making our study fundamentally different.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Metamorphic Testing</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Metamorphic testing is one of the most prominent techniques for alleviating the oracle problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barr et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Howden, 2006</a>)</cite> and has addressed intricate challenges in testing complex or non-traditional software systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Le et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
It has also attracted significant interest from the fuzzing community and has been integrated into fuzzing techniques as an extended mechanism to detect bugs beyond easily observable crashes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schloegel et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tsigkanos et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kokkonis et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
In contrast to these studies, our study focuses on incorporating metamorphic-based oracles into fuzz drivers and is <span id="S7.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> limited to specific types of bugs.
Researchers have explored the use of LLMs to support metamorphic testing.
Pioneering groups include <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tsigkanos et al. (2023)</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shin et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025a)</a></cite>.
Inspired by this line of work, our study employs LLMs to generate MRs and construct stronger fuzz oracles, extending the pioneering efforts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">LLM-based Testing</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modern LLMs have significantly reshaped software engineering (SE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abrahamsson et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei, 2024</a>)</cite>.
Many SE tasks, such as code summarization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Virk et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Crupi et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and program repair <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bouzenia et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, have explored leveraging LLMs to improve effectiveness, intelligence, and automation.
In addition to fuzzing and metamorphic testing, LLMs have also been applied to other fields of automatic testing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harman et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shang et al. (2025)</a></cite> conduct a systematic study on the adoption of LLMs in unit testing.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ling et al. (2025)</a></cite> employ LLMs to test report clustering and facilitate crowdsourced testing.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lemieux et al. (2023)</a></cite> leverage LLMs to facilitate search-based testing by escaping coverage plateaus.
Unlike these studies, our study centers on fuzzing and complements their efforts.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we investigate current practices in fuzz oracle implementation by analyzing <span id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,068</span> drivers collected from OSS-Fuzz.
Our results show that <span id="S8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">81.1%</span> of the drivers lack functional oracles for bug detection, highlighting the need for stronger fuzz oracles.
Motivated by this observation, we develop an LLM-based pipeline that leverages modern LLMs to augment existing fuzz drivers with metamorphic-based oracles.
Through systematic experiments with <span id="S8.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">three</span> LLMs and <span id="S8.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">five</span> prompting strategies, we generate <span id="S8.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">2,685</span> applicable MRs and <span id="S8.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">6,228</span> valid meta drivers, demonstrating the feasibility of metamorphic-based fuzz oracle enhancement.
A fuzzing exceeding <span id="S8.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">two</span> CPU years of the meta drivers shows that the meta drivers improve edge coverage by <span id="S8.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">18.7%</span> over the original drivers and detect <span id="S8.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">1,528</span> unique crashes.
Finally, we perform a case study to analyze the detected crashes, and distill actionable insights for the fuzzing community.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9. </span>Data-availability Statement</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We publish our dataset, scripts, and experimental results in this repository <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">MetaFOE-Team, 2026</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
